WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.166
On the left, one part on a bench. Its size

2
00:00:03.266 --> 00:00:06.356
is not going to change while we watch it.

3
00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:10.454
On the right, every number the gauge ever gives us.

4
00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:15.178
The gauge is a pair of jaws. Close them onto the part.

5
00:00:15.266 --> 00:00:19.702
A number comes out. Carry it across and write it down.

6
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.436
Open the jaws. Nothing is touched. Close them on the same part again.

7
00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:29.177
Once more, and watch where the third one lands.

8
00:00:29.266 --> 00:00:33.452
No two of them agree. How far apart they can land is one

9
00:00:33.552 --> 00:00:38.502
width, and here it is, underneath the three readings that made it.

10
00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:43.204
Take the bench away and carry that width out into the open.

11
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:46.580
A width on its own means nothing. Here is the

12
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.712
first thing you could hold it against: nine real parts.

13
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.246
End to end, that row is how much

14
00:00:53.346 --> 00:00:56.748
they differ. Lay the amber width against it.

15
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:59.919
Take the parts away. Here is the other one,

16
00:01:00.019 --> 00:01:02.661
and nobody measured a part to get it.

17
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:05.919
The same width, and a different share of it.

18
00:01:06.019 --> 00:01:08.818
So which of the two did they divide by?

19
00:01:11.016 --> 00:01:13.722
One part, and the gauge the last three

20
00:01:13.822 --> 00:01:17.636
levels measured. Nothing is touched between readings.

21
00:01:17.733 --> 00:01:22.345
Every reading lands somewhere slightly different, and the scatter

22
00:01:22.445 --> 00:01:24.737
they make has a width of its own.

23
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.942
Three levels of work produced one width.

24
00:01:28.042 --> 00:01:30.349
There is no verdict inside it.

25
00:01:31.233 --> 00:01:35.214
Answer now, before any arithmetic: can the same gauge

26
00:01:35.314 --> 00:01:38.141
be right to keep and right to replace?

27
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:41.677
Whatever happens next, the top of the fraction is

28
00:01:41.777 --> 00:01:45.221
already fixed. You cannot edit it. The only thing

29
00:01:45.321 --> 00:01:48.837
left to choose is the length that goes underneath.

30
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:54.048
Here is one thing you could hold the gauge against: how much these

31
00:01:54.148 --> 00:01:58.701
parts differ from each other. Watch the amber band while it arrives.

32
00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.838
The band did not move and it did not widen.

33
00:02:01.938 --> 00:02:05.342
The change came from behind it, and the share of

34
00:02:05.442 --> 00:02:09.284
that length the amber band covers has already changed.

35
00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:12.569
Here is the other one. This length came off

36
00:02:12.669 --> 00:02:15.931
a drawing. Nobody measured a part to get it.

37
00:02:16.016 --> 00:02:19.150
A drawing can be redrawn. Watch everything

38
00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:21.460
else in the frame while it is.

39
00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:26.120
The boundaries crossed the whole study spread in both directions,

40
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.569
and no part, no reading and no band answered. So

41
00:02:29.669 --> 00:02:33.594
these two lengths cannot be measuring the same question.

42
00:02:33.683 --> 00:02:38.124
One numerator, two denominators. The same amber length,

43
00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:40.519
laid on each of them in turn.

44
00:02:40.616 --> 00:02:45.578
Now, and only now, the two names. Two questions: can it sort these

45
00:02:45.678 --> 00:02:50.716
parts, and can it decide whether a part conforms. Neither is wrong.

46
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.298
Both of those numbers were read off the two lengths

47
00:02:54.398 --> 00:02:57.756
on screen. Nothing else went into either of them.

48
00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:00.921
The process is about to get better. The parts

49
00:03:01.021 --> 00:03:04.233
will differ less than they did. Decide which of

50
00:03:04.333 --> 00:03:07.686
those two readouts moves, and in which direction.

51
00:03:07.783 --> 00:03:12.027
The gauge did not get worse. The question got harder.

52
00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:16.648
The outline behind the band is where it stood before the

53
00:03:16.748 --> 00:03:20.472
move. And the second readout did not shift by a pixel,

54
00:03:20.572 --> 00:03:24.083
because a drawing does not know what the parts did.

55
00:03:24.166 --> 00:03:28.214
Run it backwards. Loosen the process again and the first

56
00:03:28.314 --> 00:03:32.733
number recovers, with nothing done to the instrument. So that

57
00:03:32.833 --> 00:03:36.066
number is reporting the parts, not the gauge.

58
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:41.411
Park the gauge in the corner, at the width it has had since the

59
00:03:41.511 --> 00:03:45.518
first shot, and put the part spread on an axis of its own.

60
00:03:45.616 --> 00:03:48.963
One of the two lines about to be drawn is flat.

61
00:03:49.063 --> 00:03:53.436
Decide which, then watch the parts sweep from tight to loose.

62
00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:58.445
The flat one is the drawing. It does not know that the parts exist.

63
00:03:58.545 --> 00:04:03.681
The falling one is the study ratio, and it falls the whole way across.

64
00:04:03.766 --> 00:04:08.319
Put the two printed gates on it. The falling line crosses both

65
00:04:08.419 --> 00:04:13.722
of them. The flat line crosses neither, anywhere across the whole sweep.

66
00:04:13.816 --> 00:04:17.935
And the crossings are not percentages. They are part spreads.

67
00:04:18.035 --> 00:04:21.324
There is a spread of parts that decides the study

68
00:04:21.424 --> 00:04:24.228
verdict, and it is a fact about the parts.

69
00:04:24.316 --> 00:04:26.544
Drain all of it away.

70
00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:30.074
Here is where the band started, and here is the

71
00:04:30.174 --> 00:04:34.519
band. Nothing was ever done to the instrument. Every number

72
00:04:34.619 --> 00:04:37.909
that moved during this act was a denominator.

73
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.454
So the prediction settles. Yes, and the reason

74
00:04:41.554 --> 00:04:44.932
is underneath the band rather than inside it.

75
00:04:45.016 --> 00:04:50.436
One instrument, two questions. The percentage belongs to the question.

